First, if a number has a prime factorization of the form $ n = p^a \times q^b $, where p and q are distinct primes, then the number of divisors of n is given by $ (a+1)(b+1) $. Similarly, when you square n, you get $ n^2 = p^{2a} \times q^{2b} $, and the number of divisors of $ n^2 $ would be $ (2a + 1)(2b + 1) $.

